    slug: "cnn-msnow-politico-white-house-credentials-ban",
    title: "Court blocks White House credential revocations while three outlets litigate",
    category: "Press, knowledge & civil society",
    context: "Cable News Network, Inc. v. Trump, D.D.C. No. 1:26-cv-03287",
    dateLabel: "September 18–October 14, 2026",
    sortDate: "2026-10-14",
    status: "Government appealed October 14; preliminary injunction remains the last verified relief; appellate outcome unresolved",
    publicationState: "Published",
    factualSummary: "Trump announced a White House ban on CNN, MS NOW, and Politico on September 18. After September 24 temporary relief and an October 8 extension, Judge Timothy Kelly granted a preliminary injunction on October 13. The signed order bars officials other than the President, and those acting with them, from enforcing the September 18 hard-pass revocations. The accompanying opinion finds likely success on due process, First Amendment retaliation, and viewpoint-discrimination claims. It links the revocations to objections to coverage and finds the national-security justification inadequately supported on this record. These are preliminary findings. The court rejected the requested stay pending appeal and set an October 19 deadline for a nominal bond. On October 14, defendants filed notice appealing the opinion and order to the D.C. Circuit. The notice itself grants no stay or reversal.",
    whyItMatters: "Revoking established credentials over unfavorable reporting can discourage scrutiny of executive power. The injunction now supplies continuing protection while the lawsuit proceeds, and the court has addressed speech claims beyond its earlier due process ruling. This is evidence of an operating institutional check; its durability remains unresolved.",
    limitingContext: "The government invokes journalistic standards, accuracy, and national security, and disputes the governing circuit precedents. The opinion expressly preserves legitimate national-security protection and does not decide that every presidential criticism of coverage is unfounded. Hard-pass protection does not guarantee an interview, a question at a briefing, or access to every event. This is preliminary relief, not a final judgment or permanent immunity from lawful credential rules. An appeal is now filed; no appellate stay or merits decision was verified in this review. The AP access case remains separate. No historical-comparison score is assigned.",
    subjects: ["CNN", "MS NOW", "Politico", "Donald Trump", "White House press office", "U.S. District Court for the District of Columbia"],
    tags: ["press credentials", "due process", "press freedom", "preliminary injunction", "pending litigation"],
