    factualSummary: "A memorandum dated October 7 and reported publicly October 9 establishes a three-member executive committee to investigate allegations of false mortgage statements by Federal Reserve Governor Lisa Cook and recommend whether cause exists for removal. It schedules a closed, transcribed White House hearing for November 5, provides for counsel and evidence submissions, and allows a post-hearing statement by November 10. AP reports that Cook denies wrongdoing, has not been criminally charged, and that her lawyers are evaluating participation while questioning whether the process will be genuine.",
    whyItMatters: "Removal proceedings can affect an institution designed to exercise monetary authority with statutory insulation from presidential pressure. The relevant question is whether this process establishes legally sufficient cause with meaningful safeguards. Its creation warrants scrutiny but does not itself demonstrate unlawful retaliation or loss of Federal Reserve independence.",
    limitingContext: "The President invokes faithful execution of the laws and the statutory cause standard. In June, the Supreme Court denied a stay of the injunction against the earlier removal attempt for lack of adequate process; it expressly allowed a renewed attempt subject to judicial review and did not determine the mortgage allegations. A new inquiry is therefore not inherently defiance of that ruling. The memo promises transcription, not publication of the transcript. No committee findings, new removal, or ruling on this procedure were verified. No historical-comparison score is assigned.",
